menu "Retro Arcade"

config APPLICATION_ARCADE
    bool "Retro game emulator (GB/GBC/NES)"
    default n
    help
      Build the retro game emulator application.  When enabled, main.c hands
      control to the arcade UI instead of the voice assistant.

if APPLICATION_ARCADE

config ARCADE_APP_EATONLY
    bool "Single-app firmware: only keep the eat-what slot machine"
    default n
    help
      Build a single-application firmware: the device boots straight into the
      built-in "eat what today" slot machine (arcade_eat.c) and nothing else.

      With this enabled the GB/GBC emulator (binjgb / Peanut-GB), the ROM
      scanner, the save-RAM writer and the other built-in mini games (dino,
      jump) are not compiled into the image.  The full-feature build is
      unaffected: leave this off and everything stays available.

      Interaction in this mode:
        - hold the key  -> the next picture group rolls (groups alternate
                           strictly: 1st hold = group 1, 2nd = group 2, ...)
        - release       -> freeze on the current frame (that is the result)
        - any hold time -> never exits and never powers off

config ARCADE_ROM_DIR
    string "ROM directory on the SD card"
    default "/SD:/roms"
    help
      Preferred directory for ROM files.  The VFS directory walk on this
      board is not reliable (lsfs_readdir can wedge on the card root and
      freeze the whole device), so arcade_rom.c does not scan directories:
      it probes a fixed candidate list instead, looking in /SD:/roms/ and
      then /SD:/ for a small set of names (pock.gbc, game.gb, ...).
      This option is only used by the `arcade fs` self-test and the status
      line.

config ARCADE_ROM_MAX
    int "Maximum number of ROMs listed"
    range 4 128
    default 32

choice ARCADE_GB_CORE
    prompt "GB/GBC emulator core"
    default ARCADE_GB_CORE_BINJGB
    help
      Peanut-GB is a single-header DMG-only core: it has no CGB support at all
      (no SVBK / VBK / HDMA), so GBC titles such as Pokemon Crystal animate the
      intro and then hang forever inside DelayFrame() once they touch CGB banks.
      binjgb is pure C99, MIT licensed, and implements the full CGB feature set
      (VRAM/WRAM banking, CGB palettes, GDMA/HDMA, double speed).

config ARCADE_GB_CORE_BINJGB
    bool "binjgb (full GB + GBC)"

config ARCADE_GB_CORE_PEANUT
    bool "Peanut-GB (DMG only, smaller)"

endchoice

config ARCADE_GB_STATE_IN_SRAM
    bool "Keep VRAM/WRAM (16KB) in internal SRAM"
    default n
    help
      binjgb keeps its whole Emulator (~130KB) in PSRAM.  VRAM and WRAM (8KB
      each) are the two hottest arrays in it; with the 32KB D-Cache being
      flushed by the 92KB frame buffer writes and ROM fetches, nearly every
      access misses (~300 cycles per emulated instruction measured).
      Putting those two arrays in internal SRAM avoids that, but the SoC only
      has 168KB of SRAM and it is already nearly full, so this needs the
      WiFi/BLE drivers compiled out (see arcade-sram.conf).

config ARCADE_FRAME_PERIOD_MS
    int "Emulator frame period in milliseconds"
    range 8 100
    default 16
    help
      16 ms targets ~60 fps, 20 ms targets ~50 fps.  Raise it when the
      emulator cannot keep up and frames start to pile up.

config ARCADE_CART_RAM_MAX
    int "Cartridge save RAM size (bytes)"
    default 32768
    help
      Upper bound of the cartridge RAM buffer allocated for the loaded ROM.
      The ROM itself is loaded into PSRAM as a whole when it fits (no SD card
      access during emulation); it only falls back to streaming 16 KB banks
      from the card when PSRAM is too tight.

config ARCADE_LOG_FPS
    bool "Log emulator fps to the shell"
    default y

endif

endmenu
